  bootwright / machine cp
      Copy one local:<path> to remote:<path>, or the reverse. Directories are copied
      recursively. --name takes a comma-separated list; transfers run concurrently, at most
      eight at a time. Several-machine downloads require a local directory and save each source
      beneath <directory>/<machine>/<source-basename>. An existing destination directory
      receives the source basename. Existing files require --overwrite. Paths are literal;
      symlinks and special files are refused. Python 3.9 or newer is required on both sides.
      The resolved SSH account's permissions apply, without remote sudo. --ssh-user and
      --ssh-id-file select access as for machine exec. Local files are read and written as the
      invoking user. Bootwright flags go before the two copy operands.

  Usage
    bootwright machine cp <source> <destination> [flags]

  Command flags
      --name       Select comma-separated machine names (required)
      --overwrite  Replace existing regular files atomically

  Common flags
      --ascii       Use ASCII status markers
      --color       Select terminal colors (values: auto, always, never) · default auto
      --context     Select a context (default: current context)
      --detail      Select human detail (values: compact, full) · default compact
      -h, --help    Show help for this command
      -o, --output  Select output mode (values: text, json, yaml) · default text
      --progress    Select progress animation (values: auto, always, never) · default auto
      --width       Set presentation width (0: automatic) · default 0

  More detail
    Use --help --detail full to include SSH overrides.

  Examples
    bootwright machine cp --context lab --name node local:./config.yaml remote:config.yaml
